RUNWAY NOTES
Thursday, March 26, 2026

Services revenue is not a sin

The software industry has spent two decades teaching founders that services revenue is a confession. Low margin, unrepeatable, a sign the product does not work on its own.

I want to make the opposite case, narrowly. In vertical software sold to operationally complex customers (plants, hospitals, logistics networks), a services line in the first few years is not a weakness; it is how the product gets built. Every implementation is a lesson in what the product should do without a human, and the companies that charge for that lesson learn faster than the ones that give it away, because a customer who pays for a week of an engineer's time takes the week seriously.

The discipline is in what happens next. Services revenue is fine at 20% of the total when the company is at $3M in ARR and unacceptable at 20% when it is at $30M. The trajectory matters more than the level. Each engagement should retire a category of work: the connector that took three weeks becomes a configuration option, the data cleanup that took two becomes an import wizard. If services as a share of revenue is not falling by a few points a year, the product is not learning.

Investors, in my experience, respond well to this story when it is told plainly and badly when it is discovered. "We run a services line at 35% margin, it is 18% of revenue, down from 30% two years ago, and here are the three product features it paid for" is a sentence that closes rounds. A blended gross margin that turns out on inspection to include services is a sentence that reopens them.

Benchmarks: among vertical SaaS companies in our set at $2-5M ARR, median services share is 16%, median services gross margin 28%. At $10-20M ARR the median share is 8%. The companies that got from the first band to the second fastest had the higher services share at the start, not the lower.

THIS WEEK'S ROUND-UP
- Public vertical-software companies are trading at a premium to horizontal for the first time in four years.

